maya: Quick Q — we're rolling sdk@9.0 across a few services. Two things tripping us up: token rotation/TTL behavior and streaming event rename. Has anyone hit surprises around auth refresh getting cancelled by shutdown handlers? @owen?

owen: Short answer: yes. 9.0 turns on background auth refresh by default (auth.autoRefresh = true). If your shutdown path calls the old .stop() it cancels the refresh task. Use client.close() for sockets and let the auth background task finish or call client.auth.stopRefresh() explicitly. We should add this to the migration guide.

zuri: Hit this in staging. Our worker called client.disconnect() in SIGINT and that killed the refresh; next request got an expired token. Patch we used: call await client.flush(); then await client.close({ drainAuth: true });

zuri: Code snippet I added to our handler:```js
// SIGINT handler (node)
process.on('SIGINT', async () => {
  await client.flush(); // finish outstanding requests
  await client.close({ drainAuth: true }); // don't cancel refresh loop immediately
  process.exit(0);
});
```

kai: Also streaming changed semantics. 8.x streaming emitted chunk objects under `message` keys. 9.x emits event-typed frames (`delta`, `progress`, `done`). Small Python example:```py
# 8.x
for chunk in stream.chunks:
    handle(chunk['message'])

# 9.x
for ev in stream.events:
    if ev['type'] == 'delta':
        handle(ev['delta'])
    elif ev['type'] == 'done':
        finalize()
```

owen: Another gotcha: retry defaults were tightened (less max backoff) which changed failure surface for some flaky infra. If you depended on old backoff cap ~5s, explicitly set retryConfig: { baseMs: 100, maxMs: 5000, jitter: 0.3 } on client constructor.

maya: Can someone drop a 1–2 line note in the migration checklist about SIGTERM/SIGINT handler interactions with auth refresh + streaming event rename? I'll bundle into the release notes.

